<article class="enc-article">
<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nicholas I of Russia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1796">1796</a>–<a href="/years/1855">1855</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Nicholas I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nicholas I was Emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and Grand Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> from 1825 to 1855, the third son of Paul I and younger brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a>. His reign began with the failed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/decembrists">Decembrist revolt</a> and is remembered for expansion, centralisation and the repression of dissent in Russia and among its neighbours. He took Iğdır and the rest of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> in 1826–1828, helped create an independent Greek state, crushed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/november-uprising">November Uprising</a> in Poland in 1831 and aided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> against the Hungarian Revolution of 1848. He led Russia into the disastrous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a> and died of pneumonia in 1855, succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-ii-of-russia">Alexander II</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nicholas-i-of-russia.jpg" alt="Nicholas I of Russia" width="330" height="435" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Georg von Bothmann · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Botman_-_Emperor_Nicholas_I_(cropped)_(b).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nicholas was born in 1796 at Gatchina Palace, the ninth child of Grand Duke Paul and Grand Duchess Maria Feodorovna, who was born Sophie Dorothea of Württemberg. He had two older brothers, the future Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a> and Grand Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duke-konstantin-pavlovich-of-russia">Constantine Pavlovich</a>. He trained as a military engineer. On 13 July 1817 he married Princess Charlotte of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, who took the name Alexandra Feodorovna when she converted to Orthodoxy. All seven of their children survived childhood.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Nicholas began his reign on 14 December 1825 (old style). The accession was marred by a demonstration of some 3,000 young army officers and other liberal-minded citizens who wanted a constitution and representative government. He ordered the army to smash it, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/decembrists">Decembrist revolt</a> was quickly put down. Determined afterwards to restrain Russian society, he relied on the Third Section of the Imperial Chancellery, headed by Alexander Benckendorff, which ran a network of spies and informers.</p><p>In 1833 Sergey Uvarov devised the programme of Orthodoxy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/autocracy">Autocracy</a> and Nationality as the regime's guiding principle. It brought repression of independent-minded intellectuals such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-pushkin">Pushkin</a> and Lermontov and persecution of non-Russian languages and non-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> religions. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taras-shevchenko">Taras Shevchenko</a> was exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> by the tsar's direct order after writing a poem that mocked him. Nicholas also removed local autonomies, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarabia">Bessarabia</a>'s in 1828 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>'s in 1830. An edict of 1827 required Jewish boys to serve 25 years in the army from the age of 18, and an estimated 70,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were conscripted between 1827 and 1854.</p><p>Nicholas was crowned King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> on 12 (24) May 1829. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/november-uprising">November Uprising</a> of 1830–31 he replaced the kingdom's constitution with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/regulamentul-organic">Organic Statute</a>, which declared Poland's eternal incorporation into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. In foreign policy he acted as protector of legitimism and guardian against revolution, which earned him the label gendarme of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. His army numbered one million men, but its equipment and tactics were outdated, and its commanders in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a> were old and incompetent.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Nicholas died on 2 March 1855 at the Winter Palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">St. Petersburg</a>, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a>. He caught a chill, refused medical treatment and died of pneumonia, and there were rumours that he was committing passive suicide. He was buried in the Peter and Paul Cathedral and succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-ii-of-russia">Alexander II</a>. Lev <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-tolstoy">Tolstoy</a> popularised the nickname Nicholas the Stick in 1891. The empire spanned over 20 million square kilometres at his death, but needed reform.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nicholas I of Russia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Pushkin, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=59.72,30.42&amp;year=1834" aria-label="Pushkin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5188 160 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Nicholas I of Russia"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5638 430h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5638 430h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=59.72,30.42&amp;year=1834">What was here in 1834? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
